WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.918
A pipeline that creates a file has done half of its job.

00:00:04.918 --> 00:00:07.192
The file has to reach someone.

00:00:07.192 --> 00:00:11.047
In this video, you add four destinations to a pipeline:

00:00:11.047 --> 00:00:15.280
a download link, an email, an FTP server, and an SFTP server.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.905
A destination is the last step of a pipeline.

00:00:22.905 --> 00:00:27.354
The destinations in this video receive the files that a Create a file

00:00:27.354 --> 00:00:30.795
step or a Zip files step produces, and deliver them.

00:00:30.795 --> 00:00:35.600
One output can feed several destinations, and each one receives every file.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:42.552
These are four; others include saving to the media library and sending to a web API.

00:00:42.552 --> 00:00:48.822
Share a download link and Send by email need the Email feature and an email provider.

00:00:48.822 --> 00:00:53.990
The upload steps need Data Pipelines - FTP and Data Pipelines - SFTP.

00:00:58.198 --> 00:01:01.719
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for pipelines.

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:05.836
Data Pipelines is enabled, but the two upload features are not.

00:01:05.836 --> 00:01:09.118
They add the steps that upload files to a server.

00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:13.712
Let's select both features, open Bulk Actions, and choose Enable.

00:01:13.712 --> 00:01:17.143
A message confirms that the two features are enabled.

00:01:21.389 --> 00:01:26.356
Let's open Tools, Data Pipelines, Pipelines, and edit Blog posts export.

00:01:26.356 --> 00:01:30.814
This pipeline reads the blog posts and writes them to a CSV file

00:01:30.814 --> 00:01:32.979
with the Create a file step.

00:01:33.431 --> 00:01:35.927
Let's click the output of Create a file.

00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:40.864
The list that opens, Add a step after Files, offers the steps that can receive files.

00:01:40.864 --> 00:01:44.281
Let's search for share, and choose Share a download link.

00:01:44.742 --> 00:01:49.987
Recipients are users of the site, by user name or email, separated by commas.

00:01:49.987 --> 00:01:53.779
Each one with an email address gets the link by email.

00:01:53.779 --> 00:01:56.812
A name that matches no user stops the run.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.964
Days the link works is 7 by default.

00:01:59.964 --> 00:02:05.185
Maximum downloads counts the uses of all recipients together, and 0 means no

00:02:05.185 --> 00:02:07.094
limit, so let's allow 3.

00:02:07.094 --> 00:02:10.150
Email subject and Message shape the email.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:18.567
Next, let's right-click Create a file and choose Add a step after it.

00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:21.874
Then let's search for email, and choose Send by email.

00:02:22.336 --> 00:02:27.789
To takes email addresses separated by commas or semicolons, and Cc and Bcc

00:02:27.789 --> 00:02:29.425
work the same way.

00:02:29.425 --> 00:02:33.446
Unlike the download link, these can be any addresses.

00:02:33.899 --> 00:02:36.568
Subject and Body are the text of the email.

00:02:36.568 --> 00:02:40.109
They can use the name of the pipeline, the date of the run,

00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:42.397
and the names of the attached files.

00:02:42.397 --> 00:02:45.829
The body is plain text unless you check The body is HTML.

00:02:46.302 --> 00:02:52.397
Maximum size (MB) caps the attachments, because most mail servers refuse large emails.

00:02:52.397 --> 00:02:55.511
The step fails when the files weigh more.

00:02:55.511 --> 00:02:59.552
By default, it sends nothing when it receives no files.

00:03:03.852 --> 00:03:08.656
Let's add another step after Create a file, and choose Upload to an FTP server.

00:03:08.656 --> 00:03:12.555
It asks for the Host, the Port, the User name, and the Password

00:03:12.555 --> 00:03:14.532
of the account on the server.

00:03:21.637 --> 00:03:26.070
Encryption starts on a TLS option, which a real server should keep.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:30.929
This test server has no certificate, so let's choose None (plain FTP), and

00:03:30.929 --> 00:03:35.727
the editor warns that the credentials and the files travel in clear text.

00:03:36.669 --> 00:03:41.233
Remote folder is created when it doesn't exist, and an empty one means

00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:43.395
the folder the user starts in.

00:03:43.395 --> 00:03:48.499
Replace existing files is checked, so a file with the same name is overwritten.

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:52.937
Passwords are encrypted, and the editor never shows them again.

00:03:52.937 --> 00:03:56.822
The card on the canvas shows where the files go, and marks the

00:03:56.822 --> 00:03:58.765
connection as not encrypted.

00:04:02.992 --> 00:04:06.872
Let's add the last destination, Upload to an SFTP server.

00:04:06.872 --> 00:04:10.753
It asks for the same Host, Port, User name, and Password.

00:04:10.753 --> 00:04:15.003
A Private key can replace the password, or be used with it when

00:04:15.003 --> 00:04:16.912
the server asks for both.

00:04:17.372 --> 00:04:21.953
Host key fingerprint makes the step refuse a server that presents another key.

00:04:21.953 --> 00:04:25.442
Without it, the host is not verified, and the log of a run

00:04:25.442 --> 00:04:27.678
shows the fingerprint to copy here.

00:04:27.678 --> 00:04:30.622
Let's paste the fingerprint of this test server.

00:04:31.091 --> 00:04:35.149
Remote folder works as it does for FTP, and an empty one means

00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:37.178
the home folder of the user.

00:04:37.178 --> 00:04:42.131
With the upload set up, the canvas shows four destinations, each connected to

00:04:42.131 --> 00:04:43.861
the Create a file step.

00:04:48.074 --> 00:04:52.467
Changes are saved as a draft, and runs use only the published version.

00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:54.027
Let's select Publish.

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:59.171
The plain FTP connection raises a warning, so the designer asks for a confirmation:

00:04:59.171 --> 00:05:00.384
Publish anyway.

00:05:00.835 --> 00:05:04.889
A published pipeline runs with the access of the user who published it,

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:07.259
so publishing vouches for what it does.

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:11.155
Let's select Run, and wait while the run executes in the background.

00:05:11.687 --> 00:05:14.207
In the History tab, the run succeeded.

00:05:14.207 --> 00:05:17.013
Deliveries lists what each destination did:

00:05:17.013 --> 00:05:21.481
the link shared with its recipient, the email, and the two uploads, with

00:05:21.481 --> 00:05:23.257
the address of each file.

00:05:23.708 --> 00:05:28.901
Log keeps the messages of the run, including the warning about the FTP connection.

00:05:28.901 --> 00:05:33.503
Open the run shows the same deliveries under Delivered, and the rows and

00:05:33.503 --> 00:05:35.038
files of every step.

00:05:39.225 --> 00:05:41.382
Let's open Tools, Shared Links.

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:45.698
The list shows the link as Active, with its kind, its recipient, the

00:05:45.698 --> 00:05:49.955
pipeline that shared it, and how many of its three uses it has had.

00:05:50.432 --> 00:05:53.022
The link itself is only in the email:

00:05:53.022 --> 00:05:54.889
the admin never shows it.

00:05:54.889 --> 00:06:00.732
In Actions, Revoke the link stops it from working before it expires, and Delete removes it.

00:06:02.732 --> 00:06:07.468
Add a destination after Create a file, and a pipeline delivers its files

00:06:07.468 --> 00:06:11.353
as a secure link, an email, or an upload to a file server.

00:06:11.353 --> 00:06:12.932
Thanks for watching.

